  "models": {
    "nircam-f182m": "Fitted ring, drawn in the publisher's own colours and scale. The F182M mosaic is read about the star through its own WCS onto a sky plane in astronomical units; the star's position is the observation's target position (TARG_RA, TARG_DEC). Its background, the median in the 5–8″ annulus (-1.820 MJy/sr), is subtracted so that zero is empty sky, as on the published scale. The ring's ridge, the radius of peak brightness in each azimuth under nine binnings, traces an ellipse of semi-major axis 81.2 au, axis ratio 0.878 (inclination 28.6°), line of nodes at position angle 101.9°, centre 1.5 au from the star; the single binnings scatter by 0.2 au, 0.2° and 0.5°. This stands against Gáspár et al. (2026), arXiv:2608.27437's 80 au, 28.54° and 100.39°. Depth is not the image pushed backwards: an inclined ring of that geometry with gaussian radial width 19.5 au projects to the image with a residual of 2.27e+0 against a signal of 1.00e+1, where a spherical shell leaves 2.90e+0 and constant depth, what an extrusion assumes, leaves 5.07e+0. The ring's vertical height is a stated 0.05 of its radius (the projection changes by 1.1% across the heights tried), and which side is nearer is a convention: the south-south-west end of the minor axis is drawn nearer; this image does not say which end is. Colour and scale are the publisher's: Gáspár et al. (2026), arXiv:2608.27437v1, Figure 26, F182M colour bar, whose printed ticks follow log(1 + a u) / log(1 + a) with a = 50.455, from -0.787 to 50 MJy/sr (every tick within 0.5 figure pixel), in the bar's colours at its quarters, carried as four emission channels whose tent weights interpolate the bar. Every channel shares one depth profile, so each column is spread along the ring and emits the bar colour of its own brightness. The top of the bar reaches an alpha of 0.5, the renderer's convention for a volume; the median drawn line of sight reaches 0.19. The image is drawn from 48 au to the grid edge at 240 au, tapering from 186 au, where the median brightness in the disc plane falls to the per-pixel noise (0.39 MJy/sr)."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "One image, one epoch, one filter: no third axis was observed. The depth is the ring that best projects to the image, an inference from that projection, not a measurement.",
    "Which side of the inclined ring is nearer the observer is a stated convention; the image alone cannot tell.",
    "The ring’s vertical thickness is a stated convention; at this inclination the projection barely changes with it.",
    "Azimuthal brightness differences in the image, from forward scattering and from real structure, are kept as measured; the ring model supplies only the depth.",
    "Inside the coronagraph mask the star and its immediate surroundings are not data and are not drawn; the star is the host body’s own sphere.",
    "The colours are the publisher’s legend for surface brightness in this filter, on their logarithmic scale; they are not the colour of the dust. Their scale was printed for their own reduction of the same exposures; this is MAST’s pipeline product drawn on it.",
    "Opacity is the renderer’s convention, not the dust’s: brightness and blocking are one number in its emission model, and the real ring blocks a small fraction of a percent of what is behind it.",
    "Nothing finer than the mosaic’s pixels or the grid’s cells is in the drawn volume."
  ]
}
